---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base title="About the Bureau">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About the Bureau</h1>
    <p>
      The Bureau of Almost-Happened Events maintains the archive of counterfactual
      incident reports: the branching record of what <em>would</em> have occurred.
      Every docket is a directed graph of documents — readers traverse the graph,
      and the Bureau stamps every conclusion.
    </p>

    <h2>How the archive is filed</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Document</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>caseFile</code></td>
          <td>A docket. Carries the premise, the filing code, and a review status that gates publication.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>incident</code></td>
          <td>One node in the graph. Its <code>choices[]</code> are references to sibling incidents; an incident with no choices must be stamped as an ending.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>artifact</code></td>
          <td>Evidence attached to an incident — instruments, memos, photographs — rendered as generated cards.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>review</code></td>
          <td>An append-only approval trail. Authors submit rounds; curators approve or request changes. A Sanity Function syncs the decision back to the docket.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Reading a docket</h2>
    <p>
      Entry is always the incident with <code>order == 0</code>. Choices are labeled
      doors; consequence notes are the Bureau's own marginalia. Three designations
      end a path: <strong>REPRIEVE</strong>, <strong>ANOMALY</strong>, and
      <strong>CATASTROPHE</strong>. The Bureau does not explain the difference. You
      will know when you get there.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
